comments.body:
It's a common pitfall with continuation-passing style.  Consider this:

```
function someAsyncFn(arg, callback) {
  try {
    var foo = doSomething(arg);
    callback(null, foo);
  } catch (e) {
    callback(e);
  }
}

someAsyncFn(1, function(err, val) {
  throw new Error();
})
```

The error thrown in the callback will be caught by `someAsyncFn`, which will then call the callback again.  Promises wrap their resolved/rejected handlers in a try/catch, so this comes up often.  There's a promise somewhere in your code handling the error.

